{
  "dom_id": "step-e5183",
  "name": "Example: Identifying Parallel Vectors From a Diagram",
  "problem": "Consider the diagram that is shown below. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\mathbf{b}=-\\mathbf{c}$\n\nII. $\\mathbf{e}=2\\mathbf{a}$\n\nIII. $\\mathbf{a}=2\\mathbf{e}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14251575/assets/95ae711c1ccbf7543e6d269aa8d817a66e2ac348113ea71f3a1babcbb23d1ca3.png)",
  "worked_solution": "- Statement I is false. The vectors $\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ have different magnitudes. So, $\\mathbf{b} \\neq -\\mathbf{c}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{e}$ are parallel and have the same direction. Also, the magnitude of $\\mathbf{e}$ is $2$ times the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$. So, $\\mathbf{e}=2\\mathbf{a}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The magnitude of $\\mathbf{e}$ is greater than the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$. So, $\\mathbf{a} \\neq 2\\mathbf{e}.$\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
